================================================================================

A viewer decides to stay or leave every 30 seconds.

Your job is not just to tell a good story.
Your job is to make the viewer UNABLE to stop watching.

There are only 5 reasons a viewer keeps watching:

  1. UNANSWERED QUESTION — they need to know what happens
  2. REVEALED INFORMATION — they just learned something surprising
  3. EMOTIONAL INVESTMENT — they feel something (devotion, awe, grief, hope)
  4. PROCEDURAL MOMENTUM — the tapasya/plan is building toward a boon or clash
  5. FEAR OF MISSING OUT — "what happens next will surprise you"

Every 300–350 characters (≈ 30 seconds of narration), at least ONE of
these five must activate. If none activate, the viewer leaves.

Map this explicitly. Before writing each 300-character block, ask:
"Which of the 5 reasons keeps the viewer here RIGHT NOW?"

