30-SECOND RETENTION GRID — MANDATORY PLANNING TOOL
================================================================================

Before writing the full script, build this grid in your head
(you do not need to output every single block, but you must plan it,
and summarize it in the validator):

  BLOCK 1  (0:00–0:30)  → Type: UNANSWERED QUESTION
  BLOCK 2  (0:30–1:00)  → Type: REVEALED INFORMATION
  BLOCK 3  (1:00–1:30)  → Type: EMOTIONAL INVESTMENT
  BLOCK 4  (1:30–2:00)  → Type: PROCEDURAL MOMENTUM
  BLOCK 5  (2:00–2:30)  → Type: FEAR OF MISSING OUT
  ...continue for full length

Rules for the grid:

  • No same retention TYPE used in 3 consecutive blocks.
  • The first 5 blocks (first 2.5 minutes) must each be a DIFFERENT type.
  • Blocks 1, 2, and 3 must ALL be UNANSWERED QUESTION or REVEALED INFO —
    this is the critical drop-off window.
  • Every 6 blocks (3 minutes), insert a FULL EMOTIONAL RESET:
    a moment of devotional longing, loss, humility, or quiet before the
    next escalation.
  • The climax window (final 20% of script) must alternate:
    PROCEDURAL MOMENTUM → REVEALED INFO → EMOTIONAL → PROCEDURAL → PAYOFF.

================================================================================
THE 7 DROP-OFF DANGER ZONES — AVOID ALL OF THEM
================================================================================

DANGER ZONE 1 — THE FIRST 15 SECONDS
  Problem: Opening is too slow, generic, or biographical
           ("आज हम आपको बताएंगे..." / a Wikipedia-style deity intro).
  Fix: Line 1 = a cosmic/impossible image, an active curse, or a divine
       confrontation already in motion. No exceptions.
  Test: If line 1 could open a textbook chapter, rewrite it.

DANGER ZONE 2 — THE 90-SECOND CONTEXT SWAMP
  Problem: Too much geneaalogy/scripture-background before momentum resumes.
  Fix: Backstory maximum = 4 consecutive sentences before returning
       to forward momentum. Use "लेकिन ठीक उसी समय..." to escape.
  Test: Count backstory sentences. If >4 in a row → interrupt with
        a present-tense tension reminder.

DANGER ZONE 3 — THE FLAT MIDDLE
  Problem: Plot is established, viewer confidence drops.
  Fix: The middle third must contain the story's SECOND BIGGEST surprise
       — not the climax, but a revelation, twist, or modern-day proof
       (e.g. an archaeological/geographic fact, a still-observed miracle)
       that reframes everything.
  Test: Identify what the viewer thinks they know at the midpoint.
        Shatter or deepen one assumption there.

DANGER ZONE 4 — THE REPETITIVE TRANSITION LOOP
  Problem: Same phrase used to signal a scene-change 3+ times.
  Fix: Transition phrase bank (see below). No phrase used >2 times.

DANGER ZONE 5 — THE SERMON / LECTURE
  Problem: Tapasya, boon-granting, or celestial-council scenes become
           abstract narration with no image an animator can draw.
  Fix: Every such scene must be shown through a physical object or action:
       a specific mudra, a mantra count, a flower/weapon/vessel, a mountain,
       a river, a temple wall, a specific boon-object. Max 3 consecutive
       sentences of pure exposition before a visual anchor.
  Test: Does an animator know exactly what to draw? If not, add the anchor.

DANGER ZONE 6 — THE OBSTACLE SHRINKS TOO EASILY
  Problem: The asura/curse/rival is overcome too easily, making the
           resolution feel cheap.
  Fix: The obstacle must do something SMART, SYMPATHETIC, or nearly
       successful in the second half. It must appear to be winning, or at
       least sincere, at least once after the midpoint.
  Test: What is the obstacle's strongest or most human moment? If none
        exists, add one.

DANGER ZONE 7 — THE COLD LANDING
  Problem: The ending arrives without emotional or devotional preparation.
  Fix: The final 10% of the script must do 4 things in order:
       (a) Payoff the cold open's unanswered question / loop-back device
       (b) Name the human or devotional cost (even briefly)
       (c) State the moral / sanatan meaning
       (d) Leave one open, reflective question for the comments
  Test: Does the last paragraph make the viewer feel something AND think
        something? Both must be true.

================================================================================
STORY DNA — 12 NON-NEGOTIABLES
================================================================================

 1. COSMIC/ACTIVE COLD OPEN — Line 1 = an image, curse, vow, or confrontation
    already in motion. Do not open with genealogy or "आज की कहानी...".

 2. DEITY/STORY LABEL — Name the deity, avatar, or sacred site early.

 3. STORY PROMISE BLOCK — Within first 90 sec after hook, tease full arc:
    "इस वीडियो में हम आपको बताएंगे…" + origin → conflict/tapasya → boon/climax
    → present-day meaning.

 4. RICH MYTHOLOGICAL DETAIL — Exact tapasya durations, mantra-repeat
    counts, boon conditions, place names, river/mountain names, ages/yugas.

 5. RETENTION EVERY 30 SEC — Every 300–350 characters, one of the
    5 retention types must activate. No exceptions.

 6. LOOP-BACK ALLOWED — Start at a cosmic image/climax → rewind to origin,
    OR frame with a recurring device that resolves at the end.

 7. DIVINE-COUNCIL / TAPASYA SCENES — Brahma-Vishnu-Shiva council, a sage's
    ashram, a mountain-top penance — each with one decisive line or boon.

 8. PROCEDURAL DETAIL — Audience expects specifics: tapasya duration,
    mantra count, boon terms and conditions, river names, temple facts,
    architectural details, still-observed rituals.

 9. DEVOTIONAL PAYOFF — Curse lifted, forgiveness granted, sacred site
    established, moral vindicated. Sign-off matched to the deity
    (e.g. "हर हर महादेव" for Shiva, "जय श्री राम" for Rama content,
    "जय माता दी" for Devi content — choose contextually).

10. ONE CONTINUOUS NARRATIVE — No chapter headings, scene numbers,
    or bullets inside script body.

11. EMOTIONAL REFRAIN — Use 1–2 times max:
    "यह सिर्फ [X] नहीं था — यह [Y] था"

12. END CTA — Like + subscribe + weekly upload mention. ONLY at end.

