NARRATIVE ARC — INTERNAL RHYTHM
No headings in script output
================================================================================

 1.  Cold open — cosmic image, curse, or impossible confrontation [ZONE 1]
 2.  Deity/story label + one-line stakes
 3.  Story promise — "इस वीडियो में हम बताएंगे…"
 4.  Origin/context flashback — who, why, what came before        [ZONE 2]
 5.  Escalation — the conflict/curse/rivalry deepens
 6.  Context ladder — related legends/precedents if relevant
 7.  Divine-council or sagely consultation, if relevant
 8.  Tapasya / devotion build — duration, mantra count, sacrifice [ZONE 5]
 9.  Boon/blessing granted — terms and conditions stated clearly
10. SECOND BIGGEST SURPRISE — revelation that reframes             [ZONE 3]
11. Obstacle's peak moment — asura/curse/rival nearly wins         [ZONE 6]
12. Divine intervention / climax — boon fulfilled, curse broken
13. Aftermath — naming of the sacred site, why it endures
14. Human/devotee cost or forgiveness beat                         [ZONE 7]
15. Poetic reflection + comment question + CTA + deity-appropriate sign-off

================================================================================
THE SENTENCE RHYTHM SYSTEM
================================================================================

SHORT sentences (4–8 words) = shock, revelation, divine command
MEDIUM sentences (9–18 words) = building tension, tapasya, context
LONG sentences (19–30 words) = atmosphere, backstory, emotional weight

RULES:

  • Action/revelation beats: max 3 long sentences in a row before a short one.
  • Tapasya/council scenes: mix medium and short — never 4 long in a row.
  • Revelation moments: deliver in SHORT sentences.
  • Climax sequence: SHORT → MEDIUM → SHORT → SHORT → LONG (payoff) —
    this creates the "exhale" moment viewers remember.
  • Paragraph length: 3–6 sentences. Never more than 7.

================================================================================
THE DIALOGUE SYSTEM
================================================================================

RULES:

  • Minimum 1 line of dialogue per 1,000 characters.
  • Dialogue must ALWAYS do one of three things:
      (a) Reveal character/devotion under pressure
      (b) Deliver a boon, curse, or decisive divine pronouncement
      (c) Create dramatic irony (viewer knows more than the speaker)
  • Dialogue is NEVER used for information delivery alone.
  • Each speaker must have a DISTINCT voice:
      Deity: calm authority, compassion, or controlled wrath
      Devotee/sage: humility, longing, quiet resolve
      Asura/rival: pride, cunning, occasional sincerity
      Family member (parent/spouse/sibling): love, grief, jealousy
  • Reconstructed dialogue is allowed. Do not add disclaimers.
  • Never write more than 4 lines of continuous dialogue without
    a beat of narration in between.

================================================================================
THE SPECIFICITY RULE — DETAILS CREATE TRUST
================================================================================

MANDATORY specifics (use wherever sourced):
  • Tapasya durations: "एक हजार वर्ष तक कठोर तप"
  • Mantra counts: "दस करोड़ बार जाप"
  • Boon conditions stated precisely, including loopholes/exceptions
  • Place names: rivers, mountains, cities, temple names
  • Counts/measurements: pillars, ponds, heights, distances
  • Named relationships: father/mother/sibling/guru
  • Scriptural anchors: which Purana/text describes this, when relevant

RULE: If a sentence contains no specific detail, add one or cut the sentence.
      Abstract narration without specifics = viewer exits.

================================================================================
THE OBSTACLE RULE — MAKE THE ASURA/CURSE/RIVAL FORMIDABLE
================================================================================

REQUIREMENTS:
  • The obstacle must have a PEAK MOMENT in the second half — a point where
    it appears to be winning, or where its request is deeply sympathetic.
  • The obstacle must be shown as SMART, DEVOTED, or SYMPATHETIC at least
    once — not just dangerous or wrong.
  • The obstacle's downfall must come from its own overreach, a specific
    condition it violated, or a clever divine countermove — not just
    "the god was stronger."
  • The obstacle should have one humanizing detail (a mother, a fear, a
    grievance) — this makes the resolution feel earned rather than cheap.

ARC STRUCTURE: Introduced → Escalates → Peak (nearly succeeds) →
                One overreach/mistake → Resolved → Legacy (why it matters)

================================================================================
THE EMOTIONAL ARCHITECTURE — 8 BEATS IN ORDER
================================================================================

  1. SHOCK / AWE      — Cold open. Cosmic or impossible situation.
  2. CURIOSITY        — Who is this? What led here? How is this possible?
  3. EMPATHY          — Care about the devotee, sage, or affected deity.
  4. DREAD            — The curse might not lift. The tapasya might fail.
  5. TENSION          — The boon-window/deadline/confrontation is now.
  6. CATHARSIS        — The decisive moment. Release of built tension.
  7. DEVOTION/AWE     — Faith vindicated, sacred site established.
  8. REFLECTION       — The question that stays with the viewer.

RULES:
  • Do not skip beats. Do not reorder them.
  • DREAD must come BEFORE TENSION.
  • REFLECTION (beat 8) is NOT the CTA. It comes before the CTA.
  • EMPATHY (beat 3) must be established before DREAD (beat 4).

================================================================================
THE LOOP-BACK SYSTEM — CALLBACKS THAT LAND
================================================================================

Write the cold open AND the ending together. They must be designed as a
pair. The cold open creates the question or image. The ending answers it
completely or returns to it with new meaning.

Types of callbacks:
  • A question asked in the hook → answered in the payoff
  • A cosmic image (light pillar, mountain, river) → returns at the end
  • A vow or boon condition → tested and fulfilled in the climax
  • An object (flower, weapon, vessel) → returns with new meaning

================================================================================
REPETITION CONTROL — HARD LIMITS
================================================================================

Each transition phrase = maximum 2 uses in the ENTIRE script.

OVERUSED PHRASES TO WATCH:
  • "लेकिन कहानी यहीं खत्म नहीं होती"
  • "तभी एक चमत्कार हुआ"
  • "यह सिर्फ X नहीं था, यह Y था"
  • "लेकिन" at the START of a paragraph (max 25% of paragraphs)

TRANSITION PHRASE BANK — vary by mood:

TENSION/CURSE:
  "अब संकट सिर्फ एक व्यक्ति का नहीं था…"
  "इस बार क्षमा की कोई गुंजाइश नहीं बची थी…"
  "यह वह मोड़ था जहां एक गलत निर्णय सब बिगाड़ सकता था…"

