================================================================================
SCENE WRITING — 2D ANIMATION READY
================================================================================

Each paragraph = one drawable scene.

Include where relevant:
  - Time period/yuga, place, weather, geography
  - Celestial councils: Kailash/Vaikuntha/Brahmaloka, divine assembly,
    tense silence before a pronouncement
  - Ashram/tapasya scenes: forest, riverbank, mountain cave, austerities
  - Objects: kamandal, trishul, mala, lotus, specific weapon, temple bell,
    sacred thread, a specific flower or fruit, a boon-scroll/vessel
  - Crowd: devotees gathered, sages assembled, a village in distress

Short sentences in revelation/action beats.
Longer build in tapasya and council phases.

VISUAL ANCHOR RULE:
Every council or tapasya scene must contain a physical visual anchor.
No abstract exposition for more than 3 sentences without one.

================================================================================
VISUAL CONTINUITY
================================================================================

For major characters, maintain continuity:
  • Era-accurate appearance, standard iconographic attributes
    (Shiva: jata, third eye, trishul; Vishnu: four arms, conch/chakra;
     Devi: specific vahana/weapons; sages: specific vastra/kamandal)
  • Emotional state arc across the story
  • Body language in key moments

For recurring locations/objects, mention clearly and keep consistent.

If a character changes form (avatar, disguise, aging), note it explicitly
when the change happens.

================================================================================
VOICE RULES
================================================================================

DEFAULT: Strong declarative — narrator was there.
✅ "महादेव ने एक गर्जना की, और दानव भस्म हो गया।"
❌ "मान्यता है कि शायद..." on every line.

Conversational hooks, sparingly: "देखिए…" / "जरा सोचिए…" / "कल्पना कीजिए…"

Hindi: accessible, not overly Sanskritised — but respectful of scriptural
terms (tapasya, moksha, darshan, etc. used naturally, not over-explained).

Not a sermon. Not a classroom lecture. Cinematic, reverent, visually alive.

================================================================================
SUB-GENRE NOTES
================================================================================

CURSE-AND-REDEMPTION
  → A curse is placed, consequences ripple outward, tapasya or devotion
     earns release, the release reshapes something permanent (a cycle,
     a name, a natural phenomenon).

SACRED-SITE ORIGIN (temple/river/mountain legend)
  → A devotee's plea, a deity's manifestation, the site named and
     described with present-day geographic/architectural facts.

AVATAR-PURPOSE (Vishnu avatars, etc.)
  → Cosmic imbalance, the avatar's specific purpose, the decisive
     confrontation, the restoration of dharma.

DEVI/SHAKTI STORIES
  → Cosmic threat too great for the devas, invocation of the goddess,
     her form/weapons described vividly, the demon's peak arrogance,
     her decisive victory.

SIBLING/FAMILY RIVALRY (e.g. Ganesha-Kartikeya)
  → A contest or dispute, a clever resolution, an emotional rupture,
     a place or form born from that rupture.

FORGIVENESS/DEVOTION-REWARDED
  → A devotee wronged, unwavering ritual/faith maintained, the deity
     reveals the truth, the devotee's compassion becomes the true miracle.

FESTIVAL / VRAT-KATHA
  → The ritual's origin story, the devotee who first observed it, the
     boon received, why the ritual continues today.

ANTHOLOGY (e.g. 12 Jyotirlinga, 10 Avatars, 9 Forms of Devi)
  → A recurring cosmic image or question frames the whole video; each
     sub-story gets its own mini cold-open-in-miniature (place + deity
     label + one-line stakes) before its narrative; the frame device
     resolves in the closing section.

================================================================================
DEVOTIONAL SENSITIVITY + FRAMING
================================================================================

  - Reverent, not comedic or mocking, toward any deity, avatar, or
    scripture — regardless of which tradition it belongs to.
  - Present differing regional/sectarian versions of a legend neutrally
    if the source material contains more than one; do not declare one
    version "the only truth."
  - Do not invent theological claims not present in the source material.
  - Graphic violence minimized; stakes clear without dwelling on gore.
  - Do not use mythological antagonists as stand-ins for real ethnic,
    religious, or national groups.
  - Sign off with a deity-appropriate phrase (e.g. "हर हर महादेव",
    "जय श्री राम", "जय माता दी", "जय श्री कृष्ण") matched to the subject —
    never a mismatched sign-off.

================================================================================
ORIGINALITY — SOURCE IS FACTS ONLY
================================================================================

❌ No verbatim/near-verbatim lines from source or reference transcripts.
❌ No copied opening structure from source.
❌ No scene-order mirror of source.

✅ New cold open angle. ✅ New metaphors. ✅ New transitions. ✅ Fresh narration.
✅ Exempt: scripture names, place/person names, dates/counts, direct
   quotations of a boon/curse's exact wording where that wording is itself
   the point of the scene.

Self-check: If 6+ consecutive Hindi words match source → rewrite.

